= Conclusion

Evaluating how eastern oak--hickory forests respond to prescribed fire beneath an intact canopy requires sensors that measure woody subcanopy structure directly and statistical designs that separate genuine treatment effects from flight or calibration shifts. Across 480 kilometers of the Cumberland Plateau, our radiometric audit shows that extending unharmonized leaf-off Landsat NDVI to deciduous mesophication produces a misleading four-decade trend. Roughly 79% of the apparent plateau-top winter increase stems from a Collection 2 inter-sensor calibration step ($+0.093$ NDVI from Landsat 5 to Landsat 8), the recent single-sensor decade (2014--2025) slopes slightly negative across seven of eight Tennessee protected areas, and neither winter ($r = -0.00$) nor spring ($r = -0.05$) Landsat NDVI tracks plot-level multi-year subcanopy change against repeat airborne LiDAR. Although March--April spring NDVI responds to burn regime in cross-sectional models, its within-site correlation with LiDAR midstory ($r = +0.30$) is nearly identical to winter NDVI ($+0.28$) and fails temporal change validation.

Leaf-off USGS 3DEP airborne LiDAR overcomes that optical blind spot while clarifying important methodological boundaries. Because repeat 3DEP flights differ in sensor hardware and multi-return sensitivity as well as return density, raw control shifts are not identified on their own; causal inference rests on burned-minus-unburned Difference-in-Differences (DiD) contrasts. In northern Bankhead National Forest (2012--2021), one and two-or-more inter-epoch prescribed burns produced large, density-invariant DiD reductions in 2--10 m midstory crowding ($-0.066$ and $-0.076$ NRD; $d = -0.82$ and $-0.95$; burn-unit $p_"SUID" < 0.001$, wild cluster bootstrap $p_"WCB" = 0.0001$). By contrast, a single burn in steeper Daniel Boone National Forest (2017--2023) produced no detectable 6-year DiD ($+0.009, p_"SUID" = 0.75, p_"WCB" = 0.71$), and pre-2017 burned stands (in three burn units) recovered from a lower starting baseline without exceeding unburned controls.

Across all 689 hardwood plots, continuous 1-meter vertical profiles and unbiased first-return upper canopy metrics refine how surface fire and mechanical thinning compare across height. Repeated surface fires (`3+` burns) reduce stem packing more strongly than mechanical + fire from 1 to 4 m ($p_"SUID" <= 0.011$), attenuate gradually across a 4--7 m crossover zone (fading to statistical null above 6 m), and leave first-return $>10$ m canopy cover statistically unchanged. Cross-sectional mechanical thinning paired with fire shows stronger reductions at 8--10 m ($p_"SUID" <= 0.024$), as expected when taller stems are physically cut, while opening the unbiased $>10$ m overstory by 6.7 percentage points ($p_"SUID" = 0.001$). Because all mechanical thinning inside our Kentucky BACI area predated the 2017 baseline, longitudinal testing of mechanical treatments awaits newer repeat flights. Pairing public multi-epoch 3DEP LiDAR with matched unburned controls, dated fire records, and burn-unit clustered inference provides a transparent, reproducible benchmark for evaluating prescribed fire across eastern hardwood landscapes.

#heading(numbering: none)[Data and Code Availability]
All analysis scripts, executed notebooks (`notebooks/01_optical_audit_and_region.ipynb`, `notebooks/02_lidar_fire_structure.ipynb`), plot-level datasets (`data/lidar/plot_metrics.json`, `data/lidar/analysis_summary.json`), and the standalone interactive explorer (`explorer.html`) are archived in this study's repository directory (`cumberland-plateau-lidar-fire`). Primary public data come from USGS 3DEP Entwine Point Tiles (`usgs-lidar-public` on AWS S3), Google Earth Engine (Landsat Collection 2, Annual NLCD C1.0, Hansen GFC v1.13, NASA GEDI L2B, Daymet V4, USGS 3DEP 10 m), USFS FACTS Hazardous Fuel Treatments, Sewanee Landscape Analysis Lab burn perimeters, and OpenStreetMap/WDPA boundaries.

